WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.260
 So now that we have a socket, we are going to talk about

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:04.660
 what we're going to do with it, right?

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.900
 So the socket basically functions at this level.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.240
 Your application is saying, make me a socket,

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.640
 which is sort of this endpoint, and then the connect

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.600
 actually connects to an application on the far side.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.320
 And there's a port involved, so that might be port 80.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.580
 And this is the far host, and that could be

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:22.240
 www.py4e.org, or data.py4e.org.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:28.240
 Okay, and so the socket is solving this.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:32.800
 And the question then is, what are we going to send,

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.500
 and what are we going to expect to get back?

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.720
 And that's what we call the application protocol.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.100
 So we know that these two have made a phone call,

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.940
 and it's no different than making the phone call

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.980
 and saying, you know, hello, right?

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.800
 And everyone knows that when the phone rings

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.080
 and you pick it up, you're supposed to say hello.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:52.400
 And that's part of our protocol, so who talks first, right?

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:56.360
 So the dominant protocol that we use in this section

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:57.680
 is the HTTP protocol.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.740
 And the key is hypertext transfer protocol.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:02.520
 It's dominant, it's really easy to use,

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.000
 that's why I use it as an example,

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.640
 but realize that there are many others,

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.840
 like mail and file transfer and remote login

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:10.680
 and all kinds of other protocols.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:12.600
 Each is a different application protocol.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.320
 They all use sort of sockets at their lower level,

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:18.480
 but then on top of that, they layer the rules of the road

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:22.000
 for retrieving hypertext webpages.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.980
 And we have used these for all kinds of other things.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:27.540
 So the protocol, like I said, is like,

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:28.840
 who answers the phone first?

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:29.960
 What do they say?

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.040
 What happens if the person doesn't answer right?

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:33.260
 Can you hear me now?

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:34.600
 Those kinds of things.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:35.840
 And it's a real simple thing.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:37.520
 And all you really need to do is,

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.520
 so that both sides can agree,

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.680
 you have to write a thing that's like the rules

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:43.560
 in the middle and say, okay, everybody,

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:46.600
 as long as we all do this, we'll be fine.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:48.480
 It's as simple as picking on which side of the road

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:49.680
 the cars can drive on.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.620
 It works fine no matter which side,

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:55.200
 but if each car randomly picked,

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.960
 it would be really kind of a mess.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.840
 So if you look at the typical URL,

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:02.880
 and this is one of the things that the web innovators

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.920
 in 1980 really invented that was wonderful,

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.120
 and it seems second nature today,

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:10.280
 but in 1990, it was rather revolutionary,

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:12.960
 and that these uniform resource locators

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.240
 encrypted and included in themselves a protocol,

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:18.840
 the host to connect to, and the document to retrieve.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.440
 So this is one of the clever, clever ideas

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.160
 that the web came up with,

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.720
 because we used to have to pick a program like FTP

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:31.480
 or Telnet or whatever, SMTP,

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.280
 then we had to go to the right host,

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:36.520
 and then we had to talk to that host a certain way.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:39.500
 So in HTTP, it's a really simple protocol

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:43.440
 invented in 1989 and 1990 by Tim Berners-Lee

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:47.760
 and Robert Caillou at CERN,

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.640
 and they created a protocol that we have grown to know

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.680
 and love and use for way more than retrieving documents,

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.320
 as we'll see in the upcoming chapters.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.160
 So we're going to talk a little bit about what happens

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.280
 when you click on a page that has a link.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.440
 Now, there's all kind of fancy stuff that can go on,

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.000
 but this is the basics.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.040
 And so let's just imagine for the moment

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.280
 you start sitting looking at a web page,

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:14.400
 drchuck.com/page1, and inside that, there is a hyperlink.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:18.600
 It is a indication that says when you click on this page,

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:21.000
 go to a different page, and in that,

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.060
 you see the name of the page that you're supposed to go to.

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:30.520
 So we click on this link, and that is a browser.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:33.780
 This is an application, this is a process,

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:38.000
 or an app that's running on your computer.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:39.560
 This is the browser, okay?

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:42.860
 And when the browser sees the click inside your computer,

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:45.560
 then the browser makes a connection

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:49.180
 to port 80 on the web server, drchuck.com,

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:51.520
 and sends the request.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:55.640
 This request that it sends is precisely specified

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:58.000
 by a standard, which we will see in a second.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.440
 Then the web server does some magic work.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.320
 Oops, let's go back.

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:05.400
 Then the web server does some magic work in here,

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.080
 reads some files, runs some code, does whatever,

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:12.680
 constructs an answer to our phone call, and sends it back.

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:15.240
 And it sends, in this case, back a web page

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:18.920
 in the format of HTML, the hypertext markup link,

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:20.980
 which is different than HTTP,

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.920
 which is the protocol that we're exchanging.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:25.300
 HTML is the format of the document we're getting back.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:28.080
 And in this has an anchor tag, href,

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.520
 and the end of anchor tag, and some highlighted text.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:33.180
 And now your browser gets this back,

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:36.060
 and then renders it according to the rules of HTML,

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:38.320
 and CSS, and JavaScript, et cetera,

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:40.560
 parses it, and then makes a pretty web page.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.480
 And this web page happens to have a link

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.440
 back to the first page, and if you click there,

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.840
 it will do this over and over and over again.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:49.360
 And that is the request-response cycle.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.640
 And that's governed by a series of internet standards.

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.960
 These are standards that were built from the '60s, '70s,

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:58.520
 '80s, and '90s, and continue to this day,

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:01.100
 by a group called the Internet Engineering Task Force,

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:01.940
 or IETF.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:05.940
 The documents they produce are called RFCs,

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:08.260
 which stands for Request for Comments.

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:13.720
 The RFC, the word RFC is kind of like a sort of joke,

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:18.720
 as it were, it's a, they're trying to be kind of funny,

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:22.400
 in that, funny is not the right word, it's ironic,

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:23.480
 in that they're trying to say,

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:25.400
 even so in the protocols of the internet

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:27.960
 that we've used for several decades,

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:29.900
 they're always interested in improvements,

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:31.400
 and that's what the RFC stands for,

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:33.720
 and they're all named RFC dash whatever.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:36.040
 And if we were going to cruise around,

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:41.040
 we could find some various RFCs, and this is RFC 2616.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.800
 It might have been revised since then,

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.560
 but this is like a document, and this is what they look like.

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:48.240
 Hypertext Transfer Protocol, version one.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:49.320
 And so you're reading this document,

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:50.400
 you're going to write a browser,

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:53.640
 and you want to talk to the application protocol that is HTTP.

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:55.420
 This is one of many documents

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:58.420
 that helps define what HTTP is.

00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:00.040
 So if you look down, and you look down and say,

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:01.560
 oh, here's what a request looks like,

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:04.120
 this is how I'm going to get a document from the server,

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:06.000
 and you keep reading, and you keep reading,

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.340
 and it says, you're supposed to have the request method

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:13.820
 with a space, with the request URI, the Crest method,

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:16.920
 with a space, with the URI, with a space, the HTTP version,

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:18.200
 and the carriage return and the line feed.

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:19.640
 That's what it's saying.

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:21.960
 And so it looks kind of like this, right?

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:25.120
 We say get the document followed by a space.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:26.160
 There got to be one space.

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:29.800
 You do two spaces, and it's going to be quite frustrating,

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:34.120
 okay, and so this is an example that you can run

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:39.120
 on a number of, on Linux operating systems,

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:43.520
 and Macintosh operating systems with no changes.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:46.040
 If you install Telnet on your Windows box,

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:48.560
 you should be able to run something like this as well.

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:53.320
 So Telnet is a program that we used in the old days.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:54.960
 It used to be how we logged into servers,

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:57.080
 but because it doesn't encrypt your data back and forth,

00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:00.380
 we don't use it anymore, but it basically is a program

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:04.820
 that can open a socket to a host on a port,

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:07.520
 and I'm saying Telnet to this host on port 80,

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:09.280
 and at this point, I am connected,

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:10.800
 and whatever I type on my keyboard

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:12.600
 is going to be sent to that server.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:13.440
 Now if you're doing this,

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:16.320
 you probably want to cut and paste this really fast,

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:17.800
 because if you take too long,

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:19.800
 most web servers will be like, you're a human.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:21.920
 I don't want to talk to humans, I want to talk to programs.

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:24.240
 So remember to type this fast enough,

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:27.000
 and then you have to hit enter twice.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:28.800
 So you have to have a blank line here.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:31.040
 Just type this exactly as it's shown,

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:33.560
 and then you will get back to a server.

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:35.420
 If you do it right, the server,

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:36.940
 and the server's properly configured,

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:40.600
 the server will give you back some headers,

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:42.840
 and this is metadata about the document you're going to get.

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:45.800
 It has a blank line, and then the actual document,

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:47.560
 and then the connection is closed.

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:50.640
 And so if you do this, you can set this up in a way

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:53.360
 that you can run this on your own computer,

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:57.480
 and in effect, hack through the back door a web server.

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:00.440
 Now you can't hack the secure web servers,

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:03.000
 and mail servers used to be easy to hack,

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:04.200
 but they're harder to hack now

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:06.400
 because they challenge you for information,

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:10.400
 but part of the reason I'm so obsessed with the command line

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:12.000
 is this is how real hackers work,

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.780
 and they know how to talk some of these protocols

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:17.320
 more directly, and so we think of this beautiful,

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:20.480
 sophisticated application talking to some other thing,

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:22.800
 and it's all pretty, and we got wonderful clicky buttons

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:25.580
 and nice usability, but the reality is,

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:28.500
 like in the matrix reloaded here,

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:31.560
 the kinds of things that really talented hackers are doing

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:34.800
 use command lines, and they really know what's going on,

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:35.640
 and that's how they do it.

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:37.440
 They understand what's going on better

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:39.440
 than the developers of the computers

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:41.680
 that are trying to be resistant to the hacking.

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:44.720
 So I come from a long line of using the command line,

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:46.280
 and that's why I encourage you

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:49.480
 to use the command line in this course.

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:50.600
 So the next thing we're going to do

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:53.000
 is we're going to go up into the application layer,

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:55.300
 and instead of typing those commands by hand,

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:58.340
 we're going to actually send them from Python

00:08:58.340 --> 00:09:01.520
 and write a very simple Python web browser.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:08.960
 (gentle piano music)

